---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/education/foundational/the-learning-process">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the entry lesson in Education, and it is deliberately about learning before
    teaching: every teaching decision you will ever make quietly assumes a theory of what
    is happening inside the learner. You need no prior lessons — only your own history of
    having learned things.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state a working definition of learning, and say what each clause of it rules out;</li>
    <li>describe the behaviourist and cognitive views of learning, and the problems each solves well;</li>
    <li>trace a memory through the pipeline — attention, working memory, encoding, retrieval — and design around each stage's bottleneck;</li>
    <li>use the classic results — Ebbinghaus's forgetting curve, Miller's seven, the spacing and testing effects — to make study and teaching plans;</li>
    <li>name Bloom's six levels of learning and classify an objective at each level;</li>
    <li>explain reflective practice, and why teaching counts as a research-informed profession.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever you meet a claim about learning — in this course, in staffroom
    advice, in advertising — ask <em>what evidence would distinguish this from its
    alternative?</em> That question is the profession's whole method, and this lesson is
    where you first use it.
  </p>

  <h2>Think of the last thing you learned well</h2>
  <p>
    Think back to a skill you genuinely learned — driving, a language, solving equations.
    The story almost always runs the same way. Something made you <em>want</em> it. You
    practised clumsily, got feedback, practised more, and somewhere along the way the
    thing became yours. Rita Smilkstein (2011) calls this the <strong>natural learning
    process</strong>: motivation, then beginning practice, then advanced practice that
    builds a foundation, then skillfulness, then refinement, then mastery. The stages are
    not a curriculum; they are a description of what brains do anyway — and under all of
    them, the brain is physically changing. Learning means the brain forming and
    reorganising neural pathways (<em>neuroplasticity</em>), which is why getting things
    wrong during practice is not failure but the reorganisation itself taking place.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="stack the six stages of the climb" widget="order" config={lpStageOrder} />
  <p>
    Education as a field tries to say this precisely. The textbook this course follows
    (Seifert &amp; Sutton's <em>Educational Psychology</em>) opens with a definition that
    holds every theory together: <strong>learning is a relatively permanent change in
    behaviour or knowledge that results from experience</strong>. The phrase does real
    work. "Relatively permanent" excludes a yawn, a mood, tonight's cramming (which
    evaporates); "change" excludes what the learner could already do; "results from
    experience" excludes maturation — a toddler's walking is development, not learning.
    Two grand answers then compete to explain the mechanism. The <em>behaviourist</em>
    view treats learning as a change in observable behaviour shaped by association and
    consequence. The <em>cognitive</em> view opens the black box: learning is a change in
    knowledge and mental structures. Every method of teaching is an attempt to cause the
    change deliberately — which is why a course about teaching starts with a course about
    learning.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each clause with what it rules out" widget="match" config={lpClauseMatch} />

  <h2>The changing teaching profession</h2>
  <p>
    Before the theories, the job. Teaching has changed along four visible axes, and
    knowing the motion is part of joining it. <em>Diversity</em>: classrooms now serve
    learners of many languages, cultures, abilities and prior knowledges, which converts
    "teach the class" into "diagnose the learner" — a professional skill, not a
    personality trait. <em>Technology</em>: from chalk to learning management systems to
    AI tutors, tools change what a teacher plans, monitors and personally delivers —
    without changing what learning requires (attention, encoding, retrieval,
    motivation). <em>Accountability</em>: standards, assessment regimes and published
    results give the profession external scoreboards, with real benefits (focus, equity
    of expectations) and real risks (narrowing what counts as learning). And
    <em>research-informed practice</em>: the expectation that a teacher's decisions are
    justified by evidence, not folklore.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="your first month — which demand is in play?" widget="branch" config={lpAxesBranch} />
  <p>
    The thread that ties these together is <strong>reflective practice</strong>. Teaching
    is a profession of constant <strong>decision-making</strong> — what to teach, to whom,
    in what order, and what to do when it fails — and a professional is someone who
    justifies those decisions with evidence rather than habit. Reflection is the loop
    that makes this possible: plan, act, observe the result, revise. Donald Schön called
    the practitioner's in-the-moment version "reflection-in-action" — the experienced
    teacher noticing mid-lesson that the explanation is not landing and changing course —
    and its after-the-fact version "reflection-on-action". Both are trainable, and both
    separate the professional from the merely experienced.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="in-the-moment or after-the-fact? file the reflections" widget="sort-buckets" config={lpReflectBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> good teaching is a personality — some people are
  "born teachers". The research picture says otherwise: teaching practices (clear
  explanations, well-managed classrooms, checking for understanding, spaced and
  retrieved practice) are learnable and measurable, and experience without reflection
  often just entrenches habits. The profession is built on learnable craft — which is
  exactly why the learning theory in this lesson is part of teacher training at all.</p>

  <h2>Behaviourism: association and consequence</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest scientific answer to "what is learning?" avoids the black box entirely
    and watches behaviour. Its two mechanisms:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Classical conditioning (Pavlov):</strong> a neutral signal paired repeatedly with something meaningful acquires its power. In classrooms this is the emotional weather — a subject paired with anxiety or with curiosity carries that pairing with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Operant conditioning (Skinner):</strong> behaviour is shaped by its consequences. <em>Positive</em> reinforcement adds something desirable after a behaviour; <em>negative</em> reinforcement removes something unpleasant — and both make the behaviour more likely. Punishment suppresses behaviour but teaches nothing about what to do instead.</li>
    <li><strong>The classroom takeaway:</strong> immediate, consistent consequences and the promise of reinforcement still explain a great deal of habit, routine and classroom management — while telling you little about understanding.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worked example of the theory doing real work: a seven-year-old refuses to write. A
    behaviourist analysis asks what happens immediately after the refusal — and the
    answer is often that the child escapes the task (negative reinforcement of the
    refusal: the behaviour "works"), gets attention, or both. The intervention redesigns
    the consequences: break the task into steps small enough to complete, reinforce
    completion immediately (praise, a token, a checked box), and refuse to let refusal
    produce escape — the refusal stops "paying" and the writing starts to. This is
    standard, evidence-based classroom practice, built entirely from Skinner's
    vocabulary. Schedules matter too: reinforcement that is <em>intermittent</em> (every
    so often, not every time) produces behaviour more resistant to extinction than
    constant reward — the mechanic behind both healthy practice habits and slot machines.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="bet before the intervention — what does refusal earn?" widget="predict" config={lpRefusalPredict} />
  <p>
    Behaviourism earned its influence by being measurable: define the behaviour, deliver
    the consequence, watch the behaviour change. Its blind spot is equally instructive.
    A pupil can emit the correct response for the wrong reason, and two pupils responding
    identically may hold completely different understandings — differences behaviourism
    cannot even express, because everything unobservable was out of scope. That
    limitation is precisely what drove the cognitive turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Cognitivism: the memory pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    The cognitive view asks what happens <em>inside</em>, and its backbone is a pipeline
    model of memory: information passes through several stages, and each stage has its
    own bottleneck. Walk the five panels below in order — they are the whole cognitive
    story in miniature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="sensory register → working memory → long-term memory — where learning lives or dies" widget="equationStep" config={memoryCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each panel names a different way learning dies.
    Panel 1: most information never gets in — attention is the gate, and a classroom
    competing with phones is a battle for that gate. Panel 2: what gets in sits in a
    tiny workspace (roughly 7 ± 2 familiar items, fewer when new) and evaporates in
    seconds without rehearsal — the teacher's saddest outcome, something "explained"
    and instantly gone. Panel 3: survival requires encoding — chunking and connecting
    to what is known (a 10-digit number is 10 strangers; a phone number is one chunk).
    Panel 4: encoded material joins <em>schemas</em>, mental frameworks that make
    experts efficient — a chess master remembers a few meaningful patterns, not 32
    pieces. Panel 5: learning is not storage but <em>retrieval</em>, and every
    successful recall re-entrenches the memory.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the pipeline's bottlenecks" widget="cloze" config={lpPipelineCloze} />
  <p>
    Overarching the pipeline sits <strong>metacognition</strong> — knowing what you know
    and how you learn. Metacognitive learners plan (what do I need to do?), monitor (is
    this making sense? can I explain it?) and evaluate (what worked? what will I
    change?), and the research consensus is that teaching these habits is itself a
    learning strategy with effects as large as any content intervention. The classroom
    implication is humble and profound: a teacher's job includes building the student's
    supervisor, not just filling the student's storehouse.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "I studied — I re-read the chapter and highlighted
  it." Re-reading creates fluency, and fluency feels like learning, but it is mostly an
  illusion: recognising words on a page is not retrieving them from memory. Controlled
  comparisons consistently find that recalling material with the book closed (self-
  testing, flashcards, writing what you remember) beats re-reading for durable
  retention — the <em>testing effect</em>, one of psychology's most replicated results.
  Re-reading is the default because it is comfortable; retrieval works because it is
  effortful.</p>

  <h2>The forgetting curve is brutal — and bendable</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="Ebbinghaus (1885): retention of learned material over time (1 hour to 744 hours)" widget="plotter" config={ebbinghausPlot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the fall is front-loaded. In 1885 Hermann Ebbinghaus
    memorised nonsense syllables and tested himself at intervals: within an hour only 44%
    survived, a day later 33%, after a month about 21%. The plotted line is his original
    data, and its slope is not a law of nature but a warning: <em>without consolidation
    and rehearsal, most of what is learned today is gone tomorrow.</em>
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the magnitude — the month-out curve" widget="estimate" config={lpEbbingEstimate} />
  <p>
    Two levers bend the curve. <strong>Spacing</strong>: the same total review time
    distributed across days beats massed practice — Ebbinghaus himself found spaced
    repetitions far more efficient, and a century of research confirms it across
    materials and ages. <strong>Retrieval practice</strong>: recalling material (closed
    book) is what consolidates it, so quizzes are learning events, not just measurement.
    Add meaningful encoding — the third lever — and the forgetting curve of a real
    classroom can look nothing like Ebbinghaus's, which is exactly the point: his
    nonsense syllables had no meaning, no schema and no review, so his curve is the
    floor, not the ceiling.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "cramming works — it got me through last time."
  Cramming exploits the fact that performance and learning are different things. Massed
  practice can produce a good result <em>tomorrow</em> while leaving almost nothing a
  month later — the curve above, compressed into one night. Spaced practice looks worse
  in the moment (more forgetting between sessions means harder, less fluent practice)
  and wins everywhere that matters: retention a month out, transfer to new problems,
  and durability across a course. If the exam is the last time you need the material,
  cram is rational; education assumes it never is.</p>

  <h2>Stages and levels: describing the climb</h2>
  <p>
    Two frameworks from the learning-process literature organise the climb from first
    contact to mastery. The first describes <em>how</em> it unfolds: Smilkstein's natural
    learning process, met in the opener — motivation, beginning practice, advanced
    practice, skillfulness, refinement, mastery. Its teaching moral is sequencing: each
    stage needs something different (motivation needs curiosity and a reason; beginning
    practice needs safety to be bad; refinement needs feedback and challenge), so a
    lesson plan is really a plan for moving learners between stages.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second describes <em>what kind</em> of learning is happening: Benjamin Bloom's
    taxonomy of learning objectives, first published in 1956 and revised in 2001. Its
    six levels, bottom to top: <strong>Remember</strong> (recall facts and steps),
    <strong>Understand</strong> (explain in your own words), <strong>Apply</strong>
    (use the concept in a new context), <strong>Analyse</strong> (break it into parts
    and compare), <strong>Evaluate</strong> (judge among alternatives with criteria),
    <strong>Create</strong> (build something new from the knowledge). The bottom two
    are called <em>lower levels</em> — foundational, cheaper in effort — and the rest
    <em>higher levels</em>, where you do something with the knowledge rather than hold
    it. Worked example: "list the stages of the water cycle" is Remember; "explain why
    the cycle has no beginning" is Understand; "predict what paving a floodplain does
    to runoff" is Apply. The taxonomy gives teachers a vocabulary for aiming higher
    than recall — and gives learners an honest mirror: memorising the steps of a
    process is not the same as understanding it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pin the worked objective on Bloom's ladder" widget="place" config={lpBloomPlace} />
  <p>
    A third lens describes <em>differences in approach</em>: David Kolb (1984) observed
    that learners lean differently on four modes — concrete experience (feeling),
    reflective observation (watching), abstract conceptualisation (thinking) and active
    experimentation (doing). Given a new phone, one person presses buttons immediately,
    another reads the manual first; both learn. Kolb's framework is best used to make
    sure teaching cycles through all four modes — experience, reflection, theory,
    practice — rather than to sort students into boxes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "each student has a learning style — visual,
  auditory or kinaesthetic — and teaching to it boosts achievement." The preferences
  are real (people do enjoy some channels more), but the teaching claim — the
  <em>meshing hypothesis</em> — has been tested repeatedly and has failed: matching
  instruction to a learner's preferred channel does not improve outcomes, while
  presenting ideas in multiple modalities helps <em>everyone</em>, because memory
  builds richer encodings from richer input. Preferences are not pathways. Treat
  "learning styles" as a description of taste, not of mechanics — and be suspicious of
  any scheme that sorts children into fixed boxes.</p>

  <h2>What the views share — and where constructivism enters</h2>
  <p>
    Behaviourism says: engineer the environment and consequences. Cognitivism says:
    engineer attention, encoding and retrieval. Neither is "wrong" — they answer
    different questions, and the professional stance is to know which problem you are
    solving: a disruption habit is a consequence problem; a persistent misconception is
    a knowledge-structure problem, and more gold stars will not fix it. A third
    tradition completes the modern picture: <strong>social constructivism</strong>
    (Vygotsky) argues that learning is fundamentally social — we internalise what we
    first do with help. Its key term, the <em>zone of proximal development</em>, is the
    gap between what a learner can do alone and what they can do with support; good
    teaching lives in that gap (<em>scaffolding</em>: help that is gradually removed),
    and it explains why pairs, discussion and worked examples so often outperform
    solitary practice. Learning, in all three views, is <em>a durable change produced
    by experience</em>; the argument is only over where to look for it — and the mature
    practitioner borrows from all three shelves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> learning is a durable change caused by experience; three
    families of theory locate it in consequences, mental machinery, and social support;
    and the memory pipeline plus the classic results give you levers you can actually
    pull. What remains is to make the practitioner's use of those levers concrete.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A classmate says, "I don't need to revise — I understood the lecture perfectly when I heard it." Diagnose the error using the memory pipeline.</li>
    <li>Classify each objective at a Bloom's level: (a) define "negative reinforcement"; (b) design a reinforcement plan for a pupil who refuses to write; (c) judge whether reward or punishment better fixes a disruption habit.</li>
    <li>Why can a pupil respond correctly and still not have learned — and which theory's blind spot is this?</li>
    <li>Your school proposes grouping students by VAK learning-style inventories for all lessons. What does the evidence say, and what would you propose instead?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Understanding in the moment is working memory, not
    learning — without encoding and spaced retrieval, Ebbinghaus's curve applies and
    most of it will be gone within days. The fix is retrieval, not more listening.
    (2) (a) Remember; (b) Create (designing something new from knowledge — Apply's
    cousin at the top); (c) Evaluate. (3) Identical behaviour can hide different
    understanding — the correct response may be a trained habit rather than knowledge;
    this is behaviourism's blind spot, the very thing that drove the cognitive turn.
    (4) The meshing hypothesis has repeatedly failed controlled tests; fixed groups
    would cap students by preference rather than build their range. Propose instead
    multi-modal teaching for all, with varied practice — and spend the inventory money
    on retrieval materials.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Learning is a relatively permanent change in behaviour or knowledge resulting from experience — the definition's clauses exclude moods, maturation and cramming.</li>
    <li>Behaviourism (conditioning, reinforcement) explains habit and consequences; cognitivism (the memory pipeline) explains understanding; constructivism (Vygotsky's zone of proximal development) explains learning with help.</li>
    <li>Attention gates the pipeline; working memory holds about 7 ± 2 items; encoding by chunking and meaning is survival; retrieval is what makes learning stick.</li>
    <li>Ebbinghaus's curve is the floor: without spacing, retrieval and meaning, most learning evaporates within days.</li>
    <li>Performance and learning are different — cramming shows one and destroys the other.</li>
    <li>Bloom's six levels — Remember, Understand, Apply, Analyse, Evaluate, Create — classify the kind of learning an objective demands.</li>
    <li>Learning-style matching (VAK) lacks evidence; multi-modal teaching helps everyone.</li>
    <li>Teaching is a research-informed profession: reflective practice — plan, act, observe, revise — is what turns experience into expertise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The pipeline explains the machinery but not the fuel: the next lessons move to
    <em>student development</em> — how learners' capacities change with age — and then
    <em>student motivation</em>, which supplies the energy the whole pipeline consumes.
    Later lessons put the theory to work in classrooms: managing the environment that
    behaviourism describes, planning instruction that respects working memory, and
    building assessments that are retrieval practice rather than mere measurement —
    which is the testing effect, institutionalised.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The frontier questions make the stakes concrete. First, the
    <strong>expertise-reversal problem</strong>: the scaffolding novices need (worked
    examples, reduced choice) actually <em>hurts</em> experts, whose schemas are ready
    for challenge — so optimal design depends on the learner's prior knowledge, which is
    why "what works" is always conditional and why the reflective practitioner measures
    rather than assumes. Second, the <strong>desirable difficulties</strong> programme
    (Bjork): conditions that slow visible learning during practice — spacing,
    interleaving related topics, varied problems — improve durable retention, while
    conditions that feel smooth and successful (blocked practice, immediate feedback on
    everything) often evaporate. Learners' judgements of their own learning are
    therefore systematically unreliable — the empirical case for metacognitive
    training. Third, the neuroscience layer: memory consolidation happens substantially
    during sleep and through hippocampal–cortical reorganisation over weeks, which
    gives the spacing effect a physical basis and turns "sleep before the test" from
    advice into mechanism. Ebbinghaus's curve, Miller's seven, Vygotsky's zone: three
    results, three different methods (self-experiment, laboratory experiment,
    pedagogical observation), one converging conclusion — learning is lawful enough to
    be engineered, and the engineering is the profession.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final worked case shows the two halves of this lesson — the profession and the
    process — operating as one. Suppose a class consistently fails to retain the last
    quarter of each unit's material. The reflective practitioner resists the reflex
    answer ("revise harder") and diagnoses through the theory. A <em>behaviourist</em>
    check: is there any consequence attached to the early material later on — or does
    the course structure quietly reward cramming, since nothing after the test depends
    on it? A <em>cognitive</em> check: is the final quarter taught when attention is
    structurally low (end of lesson, end of term), encoded without connection to what
    came before (so it forms isolated, chunkless items), and never retrieved after its
    test (so no consolidation)? A <em>metacognitive</em> check: do students' own
    judgements of mastery mislead them there — do they rate material they have merely
    re-read as learned? Each check yields a different intervention (spaced retrieval
    quizzes that reopen old material; linking each new topic explicitly to the
    previous; brief self-testing before study). The lesson of the case is the lesson of
    the whole unit: the models are the diagnostic instruments, the reflective stance is
    the hand that uses them — and the pairing, not either half alone, is what
    "research-informed teaching" means in practice.
  </p>

